    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Rules for The Idle Year published by William Brisbane Dick (1883) and Tarbart (1905). Wood & Goddard (1940) recorded it as Tower of Babel. The name Accordion appears in the 1950s (Culbertson, Goren). Parlett equates Accordion with Idle Year, Methuselah and Tower of Babel.',
      originCountries: [
        'England',
        'France',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1883: Dick publishes strict rules (packet must move if possible).',
        '1905: Tarbart publishes lax rules (judgment on whether to play).',
        '1940: Wood & Goddard record as Tower of Babel.',
        '1950s: Accordion name appears; Culbertson and Goren allow further deal before deciding moves.',
      ],
      evolution: 'Originally cards were laid one at a time with moves only when no deal possible; modern rules adopt deal-then-play or play-as-you-go for better win odds.',
      cultural: 'Known as The Idle Year because a well-shuffled pack may require about a year to accomplish. Odds of winning ~1 in 100.',
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: 'One player. Accordion is a patience (solitaire) game played by a single player; there are no opponents or partners.',
